<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1011"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, September 09, 2004 - 2:23 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->How about another theory, while we are waiting for a break in this case? Photography, torture and murder. Sounds like a movie. Yes, the movie was shown on Turner Classic Movies channel and I made a note of the name, The Little Soldier, on my calendar, April 17, 2004, if I was on the correct page for the day, which sometimes I am not. lol <BR> <BR>In the introduction to the film, the man said that The Little Soldier was a 1960 foreign film that was finally granted permission to be shown in Europe in 1963 &#40;torture scenes were graphic&#41;. This is when I began to pay more attention, because of the torture in the BTK case. He said the movie was brought to the United States with English subtitles in 1965. Was this movie shown in Wichita, Kansas?  <BR> <BR>In the first scene of the film, a man riding in a car is reading a book. He speaks &#40;I was reading the subtitles&#41; of &#34;Thomas the Imposter,&#34; the name of the book he’s reading, and explains to the other man that &#34;William Thomas took a bullet....breathless....die like that...&#34; etc. The name immediately reminded me of Bill Thomas Killman of BTK’s March 17th letter to the Wichita Eagle. ::shudder:: There were also scenes of a photographer-reporter taking photos of people in a park, of buildings, etc. and later of a girl in an apartment whom he thinks is a model. Definitely reminded me of BTK and his love of camera to photograph women victims. <BR> <BR>It was very late at night and I couldn&#39;t watch more of the movie, the foreboding music leading to one of the torture scenes made me turn it off, as I wanted to get some sleep that night. Since I didn’t see the torture scenes I don&#39;t know what was involved. It may not even be relevant to us because the WPD has not said what tortures other than strangulation, stabbing, and shooting that the victims endured. However, if BTK was fascinated with photography and torture, perhaps he did see this movie in the late 60&#39;s before he became a serial killer.  <BR> <BR>If anyone has seen the movie or has a copy of it, let us know what you think. TIA <BR>-Deb <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
